    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (71.71) and **Lawrence Butler** (73.47) anchor the lineup as our most potent assets. **Lawrence Butler** benefits immensely from the matchup against Young, whose 4.56 xERA and the favorable park conditions allow for a massive surge in his production efficiency (+13% from dynamic platoon adjustments). Similarly, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** remains our most reliable high-ceiling play; despite a standard dynamic platoon adjustment, his baseline talent level and the favorable conditions against Wacha keep him safely above our 70-point threshold. We are also monitoring **JJ Bleday** (62.12) closely, as his ability to leverage a +14% platoon edge against Peterson provides high value despite the moderate pitcher profile.\n\nRegarding our tactical decisions, the most difficult choice was keeping **Braden Montgomery** (45.35) on the bench. He hovered near the marginal threshold, but the efficiency edge provided by **JJ Bleday**\u2019s lineup positioning and the superior park factors for **Zack Gelof** (62.55) pushed the optimizer to favor the latter. Conversely, we are proceeding with caution regarding **Gleyber Torres** (52.25); facing the elite arm of Skubal (2.74 xERA) is a significant drag on his output. While **Gleyber Torres** maintains a high score due to leadoff usage and platoon favorability, his path to a high-scoring day is obstructed by the opponent's quality. \n\nFinally, a note on our roster depth: **Will Smith** remains sidelined on the IL, and we continue to evaluate the utility of **Luis Arraez** (40.33) in platoon-disadvantaged spots. We are avoiding the temptation to plug in **Justin Crawford**, as he is not confirmed in his team's starting lineup today. Monitor the weather in games involving **Cal Raleigh** and **Yainer Diaz**, as their scores (46.25 and 43.89, respectively) are brittle and rely on favorable park variances to maintain value against tough-to-hit arms like Cease and Scott.",
